WEBVTT

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:20.750
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. There's this kind of therapy for trauma, victims of sexual assault, soldiers with PTSD, where instead of taking years and talking, and talking, and talking on some couch to a therapist with no end in sight, you basically knock it out, all the treatment in just 10 or 12 sessions.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:39.370
<v Ira Glass>Two weeks-- you can do it in two weeks. And it's effective. Studies have shown that. This therapy's been around since the '80s, but I think lots of people who might find it useful don't even know it's an option for them. Like, for instance, Jaime Lowe, who's a writer and reporter and a good candidate for this treatment.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:54.770
<v Jaime Lowe>I was sexually assaulted when I was 13, almost 30 years ago. And for most of those 30 years, I didn't really talk about it. Not because I didn't want to or need to, but because I didn't know how. And no one asked.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:01:08.480
<v Ira Glass>Jaime Lowe decided to do a story where she would go through this therapy herself and record all of the sessions, which never happens. Like most therapy, usually this treatment is in private, not on the radio.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:27.560
<v Ira Glass>And hearing how it works, it really is kind of remarkable. My mom was a therapist. I've been in therapy. This is so different from what I have ever heard. And that's going to be our show today. We're going to hear it step by step, this sometimes life-changing process, and how that can happen so quickly.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:37.040
<v Ira Glass>A quick warning to listeners before we start about content. This story does mention the sexual assault of a teenager. Here's Jaime Lowe.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:02:04.010
<v Jaime Lowe>I thought because I didn't talk about the assault or even think about it much, everything was as resolved as it could be. But then after Donald Trump, and "grab them by the pussy," and Harvey Weinstein, and all of them, it's not that specific memories of the assault would pop up, I just felt immobilized, anxious, protective of my body. And I realized my sexual assault wasn't resolved at all.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:27.990
<v Jaime Lowe>Around this time, I heard about a therapy for sexual assault survivors, called Cognitive Processing Therapy, or CPT. A researcher described it to me as short-term inexpensive, practical, like learning a skill. The typical way to deal with trauma in therapy is to talk about the incident over and over, until it's less radioactive.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:57.970
<v Jaime Lowe>CPT does the same thing, but in a systematized way. It's all laid out in advance, and the same for everyone-- what you'll do in the first session, and the second, and the third, and so on. Each session is based on learning a skill and practicing that skill on a worksheet. You go over each element of the trauma, piece by piece, and try to see it differently. The goal is to change the story you've been telling yourself about what happened.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:18.520
<v Jaime Lowe>I got in touch with Dr. Debra Kaysen, a psychologist with a specialty in trauma therapy at the University of Washington School of Medicine. She and I started talking and e-mailing about CPT. It all seemed so different than the therapy I was used to-- my weekly talk sessions-- and I realized I wanted to try it.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:43.700
<v Jaime Lowe>I've done a lot of therapy. After the assault, I had two manic episodes and was diagnosed bipolar. I've always been very open about mental illness. I wrote a book about being bipolar a couple of years ago. But even though that book is partly about my adolescence, I barely mention the assault. I've never dealt with it, I never wanted to-- in the book, in treatment.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:04:12.520
<v Jaime Lowe>But now I was feeling the trauma more. I thought CPT might help me. I also thought, what if this could help lots of people? I asked Dr. Kaysen if I could record the sessions and play them on the radio. She checked in with colleagues-- a psychologist with an expertise in journalism, a journalism ethics professor-- to make sure we'd be able to have a therapeutic relationship while I was simultaneously doing a story about the therapy.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:35.110
<v Jaime Lowe>After a lot of back and forth and establishing some basic ground rules, we set up our CPT boot camp-- 10 hour-long sessions over two weeks in Seattle. Usually CPT is one session a week for 12 weeks, but we decided to condense it. And early studies show this approach is effective. Last October, I flew out to Seattle.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.680
<v Jaime Lowe>Session one, October 22nd.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:39.320
<v Debra Kaysen>Excellent.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.810
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. OK.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:40.960
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:52.370
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm sitting with Dr. Kaysen in a generic room at the university clinic. I fail at setting up the recording equipment a few times, before getting it semi-right. I'm not a radio person.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.990
<v Debra Kaysen>So let me tell you a little bit about what we're going to do today. OK?

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:55.340
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:03.880
<v Debra Kaysen>So usually, today is a little more heavy on the psycho ed. It's going to be a little different than a lot of our other sessions. I'm going to be talking a lot.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:04.290
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.310
<v Debra Kaysen>OK? Which takes a little bit of the pressure off of you, maybe.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.100
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:35.340
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen has a kindergarten teacher presence that makes me feel ready and OK. She starts the first session with me the way she would with anyone, by talking about sexual assault and PTSD. 50% of women who are sexually assaulted develop PTSD. That could mean symptoms like depression, anxiety, flashbacks-- some of the things I've actually been feeling when I hear the news.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:58.250
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen hands me a photocopied worksheet. Everything we're going to do, this entire therapy, is structured around these worksheets. I'll do them at my Airbnb. And when Dr. Kaysen and I meet, we'll go over them. Each set of worksheets will cover a new skill, and I'll master that before moving on to the next skill, which Dr. Kaysen will introduce at the end of each session.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:17.800
<v Jaime Lowe>The idea is, by the time we're done, I'll be able to do this on my own with any issue in my life. Dr. Kaysen explains my first assignment. Most of the worksheets have all sorts of boxes to fill in with answers. They look like badly designed forms you'd get at the DMV, but this first one is just a sheet of paper.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:37.330
<v Debra Kaysen>What I want you to do is write at least one page on why you think that the sexual assault occurred. I do not want you to use this as an opportunity to write specific details about the event, OK? This is more writing about what you think caused the event.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:55.520
<v Jaime Lowe>The phrase "caused the event" makes me uneasy, because as much as I intellectually believe that nothing specific really caused the event, there are things I still question. It's embarrassing that, after 30 years, I still might think it's my fault, but I do.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.570
<v Jaime Lowe>I can tell you that the cause is going to be very hard for me.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:02.190
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah. Yeah, we talked earlier about what are the stories we tell ourselves.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.380
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:04.600
<v Debra Kaysen>It was this.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:05.490
<v Jaime Lowe>Mm-hmm.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:28.930
<v Debra Kaysen>Right? The event happened because I took a shortcut. Right? The event happened because I had a drink. The event happened because I was wearing a short skirt. And sometimes people know that that's not why it happened, but sometimes those thoughts still haunt them. OK. There aren't any right or wrong answers. I want you to just vomit the ideas on the page.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.020
<v Jaime Lowe>That's my specialty.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:30.360
<v Debra Kaysen>Awesome. Then you'll do well.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:31.930
<v Jaime Lowe>I love vomiting out words.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.840
<v Debra Kaysen>That's awesome. That's what I want. All right.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.070
<v Jaime Lowe>OK, so.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:42.960
<v Debra Kaysen>OK, any worries that you have about doing this?

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.380
<v Jaime Lowe>Nope.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.280
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:08:19.160
<v Jaime Lowe>But of course, I'm worried. The thought of reliving the assault is terrifying. Even reading the one-sentence description of the assault during the audio recording of my book left me in tears. I rarely articulated the details out loud. I just don't know what will come up. I don't know what it's going to be like to dig in for the next two weeks.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.880
<v Jaime Lowe>Session two. So what are you actually doing?

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:23.390
<v Debra Kaysen>Right now?

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:23.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.440
<v Debra Kaysen>I'm totaling it.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.290
<v Jaime Lowe>Can you describe the worksheet, actually?

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:30.230
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, absolutely. Happy to. So this is called the PTSD checklist.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:30.800
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:37.670
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. And all it is a measure of how intense the symptoms of PTSD are.

00:08:37.670 --> 00:09:06.590
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen says we'll start all the sessions like this-- going over my PTSD symptoms and rating their intensity. How frequently have I been thinking of the trauma? Have I been dreaming about it? Reliving it? In the past 24 hours, how much have I been distressed by repeated disturbing and unwanted memories of the traumatic event? Each question gets a numerical value on a scale of 0 to 4, and Dr. Kaysen adds them up.

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:12.380
<v Debra Kaysen>And so what I'll do is I'll actually start graphing these and keeping track--

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:12.620
<v Jaime Lowe>Cool.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:13.770
<v Debra Kaysen>--of them.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:32.120
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm not sure how to answer these questions with a number, but I have the same problem when physicians ask me to rate pain on a scale of 1 to 10. After we go over my PTSD symptoms, Dr. Kaysen will ask me about my mood. Then we'll move on to the homework, what she calls the practice.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:38.090
<v Debra Kaysen>Most of each session we're going to spend actually reviewing the practice that you did over the day. OK?

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:38.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Mm-hmm.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.550
<v Debra Kaysen>So for today, what we're going to do is we're going to actually start by going through the impact statement that you wrote.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:55.680
<v Jaime Lowe>The impact statement-- the one-page worksheet on why I think the assault happened. I wrote it before sunrise this morning, and it was, not surprisingly, really hard to write.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.280
<v Debra Kaysen>So I'm going to have you read to me what you wrote.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:09:58.820
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:02.040
<v Debra Kaysen>OK?

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:29.510
<v Jaime Lowe>Uh, I was young, 13. And I don't think I knew much about actual human nature, friendliness, strangers. It's funny to think that I was not cautious about the very thing kids are always warned of-- strangers. I walked to my bus stop alone every morning. [TEARFUL] It was always early. [WEEPING]

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.190
<v Debra Kaysen>You're doing great, Jaime. Just stay with it.

00:10:32.190 --> 00:11:14.570
<v Jaime Lowe>[SOBS] There weren't many people, because it was Los Angeles and no one walks. I used to pass an alley and wave at a man. He was young, maybe late teens, early 20s. Every morning, I'd wave at him. He would walk up the alley. I would cross the alley. Or some mornings, I would walk down the alley against my mom's instructions. But we'd pass, and I'd always wave or smile, which is how I greeted everyone in the neighborhood.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:51.600
<v Jaime Lowe>It didn't seem like he should be any different. And in some ways, in retrospect, I feel like I overcompensated and was extra friendly and maybe invited him in, that my wave somehow signaled to him that this was something I wanted, that my friendliness was misinterpreted as desire. That I should have avoided him, [SOBBING] rather than interact.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:12:05.820
<v Jaime Lowe>I break down, but I keep reading. Even though I'd followed Dr. Kaysen's instructions not to write down specific details of the event just yet, they were all coming back to me anyway.

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:15.680
<v Debra Kaysen>You did an incredible job with that. You did an incredible job.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen hands me a Kleenex box.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:19.560
<v Jaime Lowe>That's OK, I don't need Kleenex.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:20.770
<v Debra Kaysen>Are you sure?

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah, I'm really into the snot.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:33.290
<v Debra Kaysen>I was going to say, I find sleeves work fine for me. OK. Is it OK with you if I take a look at it?

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:36.620
<v Jaime Lowe>Of course.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:55.340
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. So one of the things was a piece around your mom's instructions. Is that-- and I noticed that those emotions got a little more intense when you got to that.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:13:07.010
<v Jaime Lowe>That was something that I didn't actually remember until I was writing it this morning. We were never really supposed to walk down the alley, even though it was closer to get to the bus stop.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:28.190
<v Jaime Lowe>I tell Dr. Kaysen more about my mom's rule and how I broke it. She wonders if this might be a stuck point. Stuck points are the first skill introduced in CPT, and they might be the most important skill of all. In a sense, the entire project of CPT is finding stuck points, and then learning how to unstick them.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:57.560
<v Jaime Lowe>A stuck point is basically something you hold to be true, but might in fact not be true. A stuck point is a belief or a thought that's keeping you stuck in the PTSD, stuck in thinking that it's your fault, or that there's something you could have done to avoid it, or stuck in any number of upsetting or inaccurate or harmful ways of remembering what happened. They're so central to this process that Dr. Kaysen hands me a worksheet titled, Stuck Point Log.

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:01.610
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. This is going to be a living document that you and I are going to share.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:10.100
<v Jaime Lowe>We'll keep track of all my stuck points here, adding to the list as we identify new ones each session, and crossing them off as I work through them.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:23.570
<v Debra Kaysen>So I may be hearing a little bit of a stuck point, maybe, around like, I should've listened to my mom, or if I hadn't gone that way, it wouldn't have happened.

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:26.740
<v Jaime Lowe>I think it's more like if I hadn't waved, it wouldn't have happened.

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:27.500
<v Debra Kaysen>Perfect.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.740
<v Jaime Lowe>That actually feels more like--

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:30.730
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.260
<v Jaime Lowe>--what, um.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:36.570
<v Debra Kaysen>So write that down. We're going to start keeping track of these as we find them.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:36.960
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.920
<v Debra Kaysen>So if I hadn't waved, it wouldn't have happened. OK. All right.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:46.400
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen asked me what feelings are coming up.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:15:06.150
<v Jaime Lowe>I mean, I hear the word shame associated a lot with things like this. And I feel like that would apply, but it doesn't feel like the right word. Like, it feels more just like I want to just shelter myself, and I want to just cocoon.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:08.620
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:18.230
<v Jaime Lowe>I think that I also just don't like that word. Like, I think that there's something shameful about shame. It's like-- I don't know.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:23.220
<v Debra Kaysen>So I think that's going to be an important one for us to sort out, actually, because feelings aren't good or bad.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:23.940
<v Jaime Lowe>Right.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:25.220
<v Debra Kaysen>Right?

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.640
<v Jaime Lowe>Isn't there another word, though?

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:44.450
<v Jaime Lowe>I bristle at the word "shame." I always have. There's no room for shame. It sounds weak to me. Like, if you have shame, you have something to feel shameful about. With shame comes softness, and vulnerability, and fragility.

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:50.360
<v Debra Kaysen>So you will see these lovely worksheets. These are called ABC sheets. OK?

00:15:50.360 --> 00:16:27.500
<v Jaime Lowe>It's the end of the session, and ABC is the new skill. I'm supposed to fill in the A column with the activating event-- in my case, the sexual assault-- B with my belief or stuck point about the event, C with the consequence of that belief or stuck point. It's kind of clunky, but the idea seems to be to get you to see that your stuck points are not rational and that hanging on to these irrational stuck points has real emotional consequences, that continuing to tell yourself this stuff as fact makes you feel bad.

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:31.890
<v Debra Kaysen>Last thoughts, questions? How did this work for you today?

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:44.960
<v Jaime Lowe>Um, good. It was intense. I mean, I was like-- it was definitely-- I feel weirdly better, because I feel like we've started.

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:51.750
<v Debra Kaysen>We've started. All right, I'm going to make a copy of the impact statement. So I've got that, all right?

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:55.330
<v Jaime Lowe>I think session two's done.

00:16:55.330 --> 00:17:06.530
<v Debra Kaysen>I think we're done.

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:11.840
<v Jaime Lowe>Session three-- so this is hard for me to answer. Is the unwanted memory ...

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:21.860
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm sitting with Dr. Kaysen going over the checklist of my PTSD symptoms. Did I have memories about the assault? Was I dreaming about it? Reliving it?

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:27.260
<v Jaime Lowe>I do feel like I was reliving a little.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:51.320
<v Jaime Lowe>We finish the checklist and take out the worksheets. I'd done seven the night before. Because I'm doing a crash course in CPT, each session each day covers a week's worth of treatment. It feels like I'm cramming, but Dr. Kaysen tells me she has patients who are still doing worksheets in the waiting room before sessions. That makes me feel a little better.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:58.640
<v Jaime Lowe>We pick up with the stuck point we started on yesterday. If I hadn't said hi, he wouldn't have assaulted me.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:01.820
<v Debra Kaysen>Had you interacted with this guy beforehand?

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:05.980
<v Jaime Lowe>It had been several weeks, probably, of saying hi.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:11.150
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. And did anything happen on those other days where you said hi?

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:12.020
<v Jaime Lowe>No.

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:20.330
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen has clearly heard similar stuck points. She pokes holes in my logic until it's obvious that there's another way to see it.

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:23.630
<v Debra Kaysen>Could he have assaulted you if you had never said hi?

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:29.400
<v Jaime Lowe>Yes. The likelihood that that was the cause is very small to none.

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.100
<v Debra Kaysen>You were a little girl.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.390
<v Jaime Lowe>Right.

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:34.630
<v Debra Kaysen>Right? You were a little girl going to school.

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:52.130
<v Jaime Lowe>Right. But still, I wouldn't have said I was a little girl. Like, I felt like I was someone who was able to operate in the world and have conversations and interactions with adults, and have responsibility.

00:18:52.130 --> 00:19:08.930
<v Jaime Lowe>I associate the words "little girl" with a kind of unformed, helpless pink thing covered in ruffles. I didn't relate. At 13, I was babysitting. I had crushes and fantasies. I happily went to the movies or thrifting by myself.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:34.560
<v Jaime Lowe>We move on to another one of the stuck points, which was covering something I hadn't thought about for a long time-- what I was wearing on the day of the assault. I wore floral boxers from The Gap and a matching solid colored T-shirt. While doing the worksheets this morning, I realized that some part of me still thought the assault was my fault for wearing men's boxers as shorts. And I tell Dr. Kaysen about this.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:40.760
<v Jaime Lowe>I mean, I liked thinking about what I was wearing and trying to wear cute things.

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:47.360
<v Jaime Lowe>She assumes that there was some kind of sexiness attached to the outfit for me, but that wasn't it.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.280
<v Jaime Lowe>Or I didn't wear a sexy outfit.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:50.580
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. OK.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:59.690
<v Jaime Lowe>Like, I have never really worn makeup or been good at that kind of thing. It's just like not my-- like, I don't--

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:00.320
<v Debra Kaysen>Not your thing.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:00.660
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:01.310
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:14.610
<v Jaime Lowe>The outfit wasn't sexy. These were baggy clothes that masked my body. But even as the assault was happening, I remember standing there and thinking it was somehow because of the boxers.

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:37.460
<v Jaime Lowe>I remember being very, like, associating the exact outfit exactly with what was happening. I remember that it was because it was an elastic waistband. That seemed like it was, like, there was more access to me, or that like there was something about the article of clothing and the choice of it that felt--

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:42.700
<v Debra Kaysen>Like that maybe in some way caused it, even if it wasn't the sexy factor.

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:43.510
<v Jaime Lowe>Right.

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:49.820
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. So it might not be the sexy factor, but it might be an access factor.

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:53.510
<v Jaime Lowe>Or like inappropriate. Like maybe inappropriate clothing.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:54.950
<v Debra Kaysen>Interesting. OK. All right.

00:20:54.950 --> 00:21:16.610
<v Jaime Lowe>I remember picking out the outfit at The Gap. And I remember putting it in the giveaway pile months later and being asked why I was giving it away since it looked brand new. By the end of the worksheet, my thinking shifts. I articulate my new thought about the boxers-- a more balanced thought, Dr. Kaysen calls it.

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:29.130
<v Debra Kaysen>You know, you came up with this really lovely balanced thought, which is it doesn't matter what you wear. How much do you believe that?

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:36.420
<v Jaime Lowe>I believe that. I think it can happen to anyone. I mean, I think it's not-- it has nothing to do with what you're wearing.

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:37.320
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:54.670
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm starting to understand Dr. Kaysen's technique with these stuck points. She asks questions so I can arrive at answers myself. My previous belief shifts because I've concluded that it's inaccurate. She's my guide, but I have to come to the conclusion myself.

00:21:54.670 --> 00:22:04.690
<v Debra Kaysen>So when you have that thought-- it doesn't matter what I wear-- what happens to that feeling of shame?

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:08.060
<v Jaime Lowe>I think it shifts to helplessness in some ways.

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:13.530
<v Debra Kaysen>Mm, interesting. So if it's what I wear, if I change what I wear, I can be safe.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:13.820
<v Jaime Lowe>Right.

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:20.350
<v Debra Kaysen>And if it doesn't matter, then there's nothing I can do.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:25.890
<v Jaime Lowe>If someone wants to sexually assault you, I think that there's very little you can do.

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:29.340
<v Jaime Lowe>At the end of the session, Dr. Kaysen introduces a skill.

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:31.580
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. So I'm going to teach you something new.

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:32.520
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:55.020
<v Jaime Lowe>Finding evidence for whether your stuck point is true. She hands me something called the Challenging Questions Worksheet. The questions are like the ones she asked me during the session, like am I looking at this in an exaggerated way? Am I looking at the whole picture? Am I basing things on facts or feelings? My homework tonight is to do seven more worksheets.

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:57.490
<v Debra Kaysen>All right, I'm going to go make you a bunch of copies of that.

00:22:57.490 --> 00:23:00.620
<v Jaime Lowe>OK. And I am going to push Stop.

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:11.290
<v Debra Kaysen>Excellent.

00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:12.890
<v Jaime Lowe>Session four.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.930
<v Debra Kaysen>So first of all, how is your mood?

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:18.370
<v Jaime Lowe>You know, I don't know how people live in Seattle.

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:18.950
<v Debra Kaysen>[LAUGHS]

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:21.240
<v Jaime Lowe>It's very gray and rainy.

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:21.550
<v Debra Kaysen>Yes.

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:25.630
<v Jaime Lowe>Like all morning, I really wanted to take a walk.

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:45.040
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm not used to the gray, damp fog of Seattle. I understand the moodiness of "Twin Peaks" better and why cardigans were so essential for Kurt Cobain. It doesn't help that the majority of my time is spent alone, holed up making spaghetti and doing homework, piles and piles of homework.

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:46.870
<v Debra Kaysen>How was the practice?

00:23:46.870 --> 00:23:55.160
<v Jaime Lowe>It was harder than the other practice. I think it's also I'm just so used to knowing what therapy is.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:55.540
<v Debra Kaysen>Is it really different?

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:57.430
<v Jaime Lowe>It's like, it's super different.

00:23:57.430 --> 00:23:59.360
<v Debra Kaysen>It is super different.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:07.740
<v Jaime Lowe>And so I guess I feel like a straight A student in English who's taking a chemistry class. Like, I'm in someone else's clothes.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:08.440
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah. Yeah.

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:10.180
<v Debra Kaysen>And they aren't quite fitting.

00:24:10.180 --> 00:24:11.620
<v Debra Kaysen>You're in a different major right now.

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:12.790
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:24:12.790 --> 00:24:22.390
<v Jaime Lowe>I take out the worksheets, and we loop back to my stuck point about the boxers. I wondered if it would have been harder for him to assault me if I was wearing jeans.

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:31.360
<v Debra Kaysen>And from what you know about perpetration, more difficult, does that mean not possible?

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.070
<v Jaime Lowe>No, it's still possible.

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:35.130
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah. And he had a knife, right?

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:35.780
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:48.160
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah. OK. And we're going to be working on a skill tomorrow. It looks like you're having some strong feelings as we're working on this one. What's coming up for you right now?

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:52.500
<v Jaime Lowe>[SNIFFS] [SOBS]

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:53.890
<v Debra Kaysen>Stay with it.

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:58.940
<v Jaime Lowe>I think just thinking about the knife always makes me really emotional.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:12.870
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah. So I want you to not try and shut the emotions down, OK? Walk me through how it unfolded.

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:18.140
<v Jaime Lowe>[SIGHS] I was walking to school.

00:25:18.140 --> 00:25:18.410
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:22.550
<v Jaime Lowe>I walked past the alley.

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:37.850
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm not used to speaking out loud about what happened, but I knew that at some point it would come up. It would all come up-- the details, how I felt, what exactly happened in that moment, and what exactly changed.

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:59.590
<v Jaime Lowe>We're nearing the end of our first week together, and I feel like Dr. Kaysen, on purpose or not, has waited until we both felt comfortable. There was trust, the skills of CPT had been discussed, and then this, this story-- the main reason I was here in this room, in this city. It was time for it to come out.

00:25:59.590 --> 00:26:05.930
<v Jaime Lowe>And he was kind of in this, like, kind of cove, I guess, behind a bush.

00:26:05.930 --> 00:26:20.570
<v Jaime Lowe>We lived in a very middle class area in West Los Angeles. Our apartment was near two big streets, Santa Monica Boulevard and Beverly Glen. There were office buildings nearby and a big mall with a massive food court.

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:42.860
<v Jaime Lowe>My mom's greatest fear in life was that our landlord would sell the duplex, forcing us to leave. It was a place where I knew most of our neighbors. There were always people hanging out in front of their houses. Our across-the-street-neighbors were mechanics with a pitbull named Bumper. It was a tree-lined, sun-kissed, America dream neighborhood.

00:26:42.860 --> 00:26:50.370
<v Jaime Lowe>That morning, I walked past an alley between an office building and an apartment building, and the guy was there.

00:26:50.370 --> 00:27:03.680
<v Jaime Lowe>And he, like-- like, I smiled or waved, and then he beckoned me over. I didn't know why. And then I walked over.

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:33.950
<v Jaime Lowe>And he held a knife to my side, and then he put his hands down my boxers and felt my vagina. And then I think I said something about, like, protesting. And he said that my father owed him money. And I was like, there's no way that's true.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:59.230
<v Jaime Lowe>And then he kept saying, let me just kiss it, and put his mouth on my vagina. And then I scream, but it wasn't a scream. It was just kind of a vocal articulation. It wasn't particularly loud. And then he ran up the alley the other way.

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:14.860
<v Jaime Lowe>I wish the word vagina didn't feel so clinical. It was more than that. It was like he found a way into my personal sexuality, like a portal into parts of me I hadn't explored or known, because I was so young.

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:37.360
<v Jaime Lowe>Eighth grade-- before it happened, I would daydream about having a first kiss. And then after it happened, I thought I'd never be able to. Like, I'd never be able to experience romantic interactions or understand them. Sex, sexual bodies, that shit is hard enough for a 13-year-old.

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:45.180
<v Debra Kaysen>And so in that moment where he had a knife up to you, what did you think was going to happen? Do you remember?

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:48.540
<v Jaime Lowe>I have no idea. I didn't know.

00:28:48.540 --> 00:28:52.180
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah. You were just super scared.

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:56.120
<v Jaime Lowe>Yep.

00:28:56.120 --> 00:28:58.910
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:28.780
<v Jaime Lowe>I mean, I think part of the reason that I haven't dealt with a lot of it is like the outcome wasn't-- and I know I've heard this before from other people who have been assaulted, is that the outcome wasn't, like, that bad. I wasn't raped. I wasn't, like, even cut. Like, nothing was physically wrong. Nothing was particularly, like, damaged, I guess, except for psychologically, everything was.

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:29.690
<v Debra Kaysen>Right.

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:38.380
<v Jaime Lowe>And so it's, in some ways, I think even harder because I felt like it wasn't so bad. Or I was supposed to think it wasn't so bad.

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:48.970
<v Debra Kaysen>So I'm hearing a stuck point around, I shouldn't be distressed, or this wasn't-- there's almost a little bit of a minimizing kind of stuck point.

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:49.990
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:29:49.990 --> 00:30:05.960
<v Debra Kaysen>But how would you put it-- what would the belief be around that? The, because I wasn't raped, or because I wasn't cut, I shouldn't have these reactions? I'm not sure exactly what the stuck point is.

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:08.870
<v Jaime Lowe>I think because it wasn't worse.

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:33.240
<v Jaime Lowe>Because it wasn't worse, I should be functioning better. I add this to the stuck point log. My experience, by the way, was an outlier in that I was assaulted by a stranger. Sexual assault is usually perpetrated by an acquaintance or family member. Only 7% of juvenile victims who report sexual assault are assaulted by strangers.

00:30:33.240 --> 00:31:07.050
<v Jaime Lowe>At the end of the session, Dr. Kaysen introduces the new skill-- patterns of problematic thinking, which include things like mind reading, and emotional reasoning, and exaggerating, or minimizing. I'm put off by the language. It's oddly formal. I came in thinking CPT was supposed to be accessible, but it's hard to get a handle on. They use real English words, but not in the dictionary definition way. The phrases have a separate CPT meaning.

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:10.800
<v Debra Kaysen>Do these make sense for the most part for you?

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:11.740
<v Jaime Lowe>Kind of.

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:13.530
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. Where do you have questions?

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:15.750
<v Jaime Lowe>I guess, what are we doing this for?

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:16.710
<v Debra Kaysen>Mm-hmm.

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:29.420
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen explains that this is part of a process we are in the process of learning, that it will make sense. But I don't see how it's adding up yet. It's just painful and hard.

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:32.970
<v Debra Kaysen>All right, but I'm going to make you another copy of the actual patterns form.

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:41.360
<v Jaime Lowe>OK, cool.

00:31:41.360 --> 00:32:02.990
<v Jaime Lowe>It's Session five. I've made it to Friday of my first week. It feels like a small victory. I'm thinking of the assault constantly. I'm staying in the gentrified industrial neighborhood of Ballard. It's quiet. There are lots of car repair and tire shops, and warehouses, and expensive microbreweries.

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:20.190
<v Jaime Lowe>Crossing the street, I'm reminded of the alley. Locking the doors at night to the Airbnb, I worry about security. I don't know anyone around me, and I feel alone. I don't want to take risks. I don't want to explore Seattle. I won't go for a run.

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:25.000
<v Debra Kaysen>Taking too many risks or doing things that could cause you harm.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:25.450
<v Jaime Lowe>Zero.

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:30.530
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. Being super alert or watchful or on guard.

00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:31.270
<v Jaime Lowe>Two.

00:32:31.270 --> 00:32:32.080
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.

00:32:32.080 --> 00:33:04.790
<v Jaime Lowe>Today, after the PTSD checklist, Dr. Kaysen reveals the worksheet that all the worksheets were leading up to this whole time. It has many boxes, and tables, and questions, 26 in all. It's the mother of all worksheets. It includes all the things I've learned on the worksheets til now. It's intimidating and complicated, but Dr. Kaysen reminds me that I know how to do each step. She says now that I have all the skills I need to do CPT, the therapy will shift.

00:33:04.790 --> 00:33:13.950
<v Debra Kaysen>But what we're going to be doing from this session on out is we're going to start working with different themes. The first one we're going to focus in on is around safety.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:14.410
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:33:14.410 --> 00:33:19.460
<v Debra Kaysen>OK? So how this event may have affected your ideas about safety.

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:20.660
<v Jaime Lowe>Mm-hmm.

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:37.490
<v Jaime Lowe>We practice a couple stuck points on the big mama worksheet. One of them is about how I'm feeling uncomfortable in Seattle. I mention that the Dodgers are playing in the World Series, and I'm hesitant to walk the few blocks to the bar at the end of my street. But I really want to watch the game.

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:50.060
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen says that I should consider trying to go to the bar to watch the game, that it's actually not risky behavior. She's not a big baseball fan, so I almost feel like the assignment is for both of us.

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:52.670
<v Debra Kaysen>You know, I'm going to ask you on Monday whether you saw the game.

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:53.580
<v Jaime Lowe>Went to it. [LAUGHS]

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:57.750
<v Debra Kaysen>Yes. Yes. OK. All right. So in terms of--

00:33:57.750 --> 00:33:59.960
<v Jaime Lowe>And I'll ask you if you know what the score was.

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:04.580
<v Debra Kaysen>Oh. Oh. [LAUGHS] Hmm.

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:04.960
<v Jaime Lowe>There are several games, actually.

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:10.670
<v Debra Kaysen>But I will have to actually-- I hate to tell you this, but I'm really treatment resistant.

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:14.650
<v Jaime Lowe>[LAUGHS] This sounds like a stuck point.

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:15.790
<v Debra Kaysen>It could be a stuck point.

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:17.050
<v Jaime Lowe>We could get through the baseball stuck point.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:19.460
<v Debra Kaysen>[LAUGHS]

00:34:19.460 --> 00:34:20.660
<v Jaime Lowe>We can work together on this.

00:34:20.660 --> 00:34:23.090
<v Debra Kaysen>Absolutely. We'll set it as a long-term treatment goal.

00:34:23.090 --> 00:34:23.660
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:34:23.660 --> 00:34:26.270
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. So in terms of resolutions--

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:38.450
<v Jaime Lowe>As hard as this week has been, it helps that I trust Dr. Kaysen. She's a good guide.

00:34:38.450 --> 00:34:55.800
<v Ira Glass>Jaime Lowe. Coming up, if you learn something important about yourself from a worksheet, is it OK to feel resentful that you learned it from a worksheet? That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.

00:34:55.800 --> 00:35:22.690
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, Ten Sessions. What can you do with trauma from long ago that's never healed? If you're just tuning in, writer Jaime Lowe heard about a kind of therapy called CPT, Cognitive Processing Therapy, that helps people deal with unhealed trauma from sexual assault or combat PTSD, incredibly, in just 10 or 12 sessions. And she decided to try it herself. We pick up halfway through the therapy.

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:24.340
<v Jaime Lowe>Session six.

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:30.910
<v Debra Kaysen>All right, so in general, since I last saw you on Friday, how has your mood been?

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:55.200
<v Jaime Lowe>It's Monday, and I've self-cared my way through the weekend, which is another way to say that I went to a handful of vintage clothing stores. I bought a poncho the colors of Mardi Gras. I did not buy the T-shirt that said "Rain, and Coffee, and Salmon, and Weed." The Dodgers lost the series, but I went to the bar to watch the game, and it was fine. It was actually nice.

00:35:55.200 --> 00:36:00.350
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. So do you want to check levels? And then we will delve in.

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:04.580
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. Why don't you speak a tiny bit?

00:36:04.580 --> 00:36:19.780
<v Ira Glass>We check the audio levels. I still have no idea if I'm doing it right. I tell myself, worst case scenario, I won't be able to share the most traumatic moments of my life with millions of people. And that seems like an OK outcome.

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:32.250
<v Ira Glass>I take out the big mama worksheets I did over the weekend. Each sheet took a lot longer than I expected. Dr. Kaysen picks out a worksheet with the stuck point, I can't protect myself.

00:36:32.250 --> 00:36:38.310
<v Debra Kaysen>OK, I can't protect myself. Oh, good. This is a great one to do, also, with you going out to the bar, too.

00:36:38.310 --> 00:36:38.480
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:39.930
<v Debra Kaysen>Like really relevant.

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:50.410
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen pauses. It seems like she's seeing a stuck point in the stuck point, which happens a lot-- Russian dolls of stuck points.

00:36:50.410 --> 00:36:56.280
<v Debra Kaysen>Let me ask you a question also. There's another piece here. Are you different now than you were then?

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:56.830
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:36:56.830 --> 00:36:58.240
<v Debra Kaysen>How so?

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:04.850
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm older. I know more. I've learned from that experience.

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:07.780
<v Debra Kaysen>Mm-hmm.

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:11.850
<v Jaime Lowe>This reminds me of that song Into the Woods, the musical.

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:12.550
<v Debra Kaysen>Ah.

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:17.120
<v Jaime Lowe>The Little Red Riding Hood song, which is basically all about sexual trauma.

00:37:17.120 --> 00:38:00.230
<v Ira Glass>There's a song from Stephen Sondheim's musical, Into the Woods, that I used to listen to over and over after the attack. The wolf beckons Little Red Riding Hood. She strays from the path, and then she sings, "He drew me close and he swallowed me down, down a dark slimy path where life's secrets that I never want to know and when everything familiar seemed to disappear forever." I mean, those lyrics described how I felt in the aftermath of the assault to a tee, that everything familiar seemed to disappear forever.

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:29.210
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Kaysen and I walk through the worksheet, step by step, through all the prompts and questions CPT relies on-- whether there's evidence for my stuck point, whether I'm making an all or nothing statement, whether I'm jumping to conclusions, what I'm leaving out. I realize that I still feel angry with myself for freezing, that I didn't scream sooner, hit him, run, or defend myself. Dr. Kaysen reminds me that he had a knife.

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:36.980
<v Debra Kaysen>If somebody's got a weapon, sometimes freezing is the best response in that moment.

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:43.840
<v Jaime Lowe>Or the only response. We continue going through it. And eventually, I reach a more balanced thought.

00:38:43.840 --> 00:38:50.090
<v Debra Kaysen>Good. I did what I could to protect myself from physical harm. Absolutely. That's beautiful that you came up with that.

00:38:50.090 --> 00:39:08.210
<v Jaime Lowe>The big mama worksheets ask for percentages related to how much you feel things and to how much you believe certain thoughts. At the beginning of this worksheet, I believed I can't protect myself 70%. By the end, I'm in a different place.

00:39:08.210 --> 00:39:13.790
<v Debra Kaysen>And so you've managed to get that from 70% to 20%. That's some really nice movement around that.

00:39:13.790 --> 00:39:21.990
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. I don't fully understand the percentages, but I did feel better after working through it.

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:37.460
<v Debra Kaysen>Nice. And fear came down, anger came down, and frustration came down. Nice. That's lovely. And you've also got some other balanced thoughts in here, too, like, I can protect myself most of the time.

00:39:37.460 --> 00:39:37.760
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:46.580
<v Debra Kaysen>You can think about how does that feel, versus I can't protect myself at all. Right. Beautiful job with that.

00:39:46.580 --> 00:40:04.880
<v Jaime Lowe>Suddenly, I'm getting the worksheets. They're still difficult, because thinking is difficult. But that night, I find that doing them is calming and clear. The next day, in session seven, I tell Dr. Kaysen that there's something about the writing that's really key.

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:13.780
<v Jaime Lowe>And so when I'm writing it down, I'm just seeing that even just subtle shifts are really different when you write it.

00:40:13.780 --> 00:40:14.590
<v Debra Kaysen>That's right.

00:40:14.590 --> 00:40:26.320
<v Jaime Lowe>I can see the beginning point. And then I see the end, and I'm just like, ah. It's like you're forehead-slapping. You're just like, oh, yeah, that starting point was really off, like, that's not a rational way to think.

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:35.500
<v Debra Kaysen>Right. But I love what I'm hearing you say is, also, you're really seeing some cognitive shifts with doing these. So it's hard work, but you're also seeing some payout.

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:39.940
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. I think I'm also a little resentful that it's just coming from a worksheet.

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:40.280
<v Debra Kaysen>[LAUGHS]

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:42.250
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm serious. Like, there's something like--

00:40:42.250 --> 00:40:42.820
<v Debra Kaysen>Why?

00:40:42.820 --> 00:41:08.290
<v Jaime Lowe>Because it's like-- I can't describe it, but it's sort of like-- OK, I think I can describe it. I think that in talk therapy or traditional analysis, it's so individualized, it's so cocooned, it's so specified to your relationship with one person in that space and that time. And it's very much like a special thing.

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:17.520
<v Jaime Lowe>Whereas a worksheet, it's an equation. And so it's more generic, which actually makes it way better in a lot of ways.

00:41:17.520 --> 00:41:19.590
<v Debra Kaysen>But also hard because it feels less special.

00:41:19.590 --> 00:41:27.870
<v Jaime Lowe>Right, but also kind of like not this precious thing. You're special, and your problems are special.

00:41:27.870 --> 00:41:29.650
<v Debra Kaysen>Huh, that's interesting.

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:45.280
<v Jaime Lowe>I like that the goal of CPT is to have the tools to be your own therapist. Several CPT experts told me their role as clinicians was to put themselves out of business. I'm not sure that's true of talk therapy-- for me, anyway.

00:41:45.280 --> 00:42:10.060
<v Jaime Lowe>I don't set an end goal. With CPT, I have. I know exactly what I want to work on, and I'm drilling down on that one thing. I'm dealing with the after effects of sexual assault. And I have a list of stuck points to show for it-- 31 different sentences written sloppily on a piece of paper, like a list of groceries. Dr. Kaysen asks me if I've crossed any off.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:18.190
<v Jaime Lowe>I crossed off a bunch that I just didn't think were stuck points in the first place. And then I crossed off some that I felt like we worked through.

00:42:18.190 --> 00:42:19.420
<v Debra Kaysen>That's fantastic.

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:20.290
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:42:20.290 --> 00:42:41.770
<v Ira Glass>If I hadn't said hi, he wouldn't have assaulted me-- done with that. I can't trust my judgment-- crossed off. Because it wasn't worse, I should be functioning better-- gone. I've been working so hard to learn the process of CPT that I didn't realize how much I'd resolved along the way.

00:42:41.770 --> 00:42:57.800
<v Ira Glass>I leave the session feeling a sense of accomplishment. I love making lists. I love crossing things off my lists. This is starting to feel satisfying.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:42:59.120
<v Jaime Lowe>You check my levels, and I'll check yours--

00:42:59.120 --> 00:43:00.310
<v Debra Kaysen>And then you can check my levels.

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:00.490
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:43:00.490 --> 00:43:01.560
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:10.340
<v Jaime Lowe>It's session eight, and Dr. Kaysen and I are making levels jokes. I'll check the level of her volume, and she'll check the levels of my PTSD symptoms.

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:19.110
<v Debra Kaysen>So since yesterday, how much have you been distressed by repeated disturbing and unwanted memories of the traumatic event?

00:43:19.110 --> 00:43:20.460
<v Jaime Lowe>Two-- eh, one.

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:21.020
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.

00:43:21.020 --> 00:43:22.210
<v Jaime Lowe>Probably one.

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:30.560
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Kaysen and I are starting to get into a nice, comfortable rhythm. And of course, there's only three sessions left, including this one.

00:43:30.560 --> 00:43:44.870
<v Ira Glass>It's Halloween, and she's got on themed earrings and spider web tights. In another setting, I might find this kind of ridiculous, but I know her enough to know it's genuine and wonderful. And I feel sad I'm not dressed up, too.

00:43:44.870 --> 00:44:05.850
<v Ira Glass>We go through my big mama worksheets. Just as Dr. Kaysen said we would, we've been using the big worksheets to explore big themes. We've gone through safety and esteem. Today is control. And one of my stuck points is, when I'm not in control, bad things will happen, which I believe 80%.

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:12.470
<v Debra Kaysen>So what evidence do you have that, if you are not in control, bad things will happen?

00:44:12.470 --> 00:44:15.620
<v Jaime Lowe>Bad things have happened when I haven't been in control.

00:44:15.620 --> 00:44:20.670
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. So you could say things in the past.

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:21.360
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:30.790
<v Debra Kaysen>OK? So I'm assuming like the sexual assault, events that happen when you are manic. OK. OK.

00:44:30.790 --> 00:44:56.980
<v Jaime Lowe>When Dr. Kaysen asks about mania, I pause, just sit there for a while. Then I nod without saying anything. Control is a big thing for me. It's a big thing in mania. You're not in control, but you think you are. Dr. Kaysen and I keep going through this sheet. My statement, when I'm not in control, bad things will happen-- is there evidence it might not be true?

00:44:56.980 --> 00:44:59.460
<v Debra Kaysen>So bad things can happen when--

00:44:59.460 --> 00:45:00.720
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm in control.

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:07.340
<v Debra Kaysen>When you are in control-- yeah, absolutely. And have you ever been out of control and not had something bad happen?

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:08.830
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:45:08.830 --> 00:45:27.700
<v Ira Glass>We go over my answers to each of the little boxes on the worksheet. By the end, I have a new thought. Giving up control does not always mean bad things will happen. Dr. Kaysen asks, what feelings come up when I'm thinking about giving up control? Fear is the first one. And then--

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:29.780
<v Jaime Lowe>There's definitely a lot of guilt and shame.

00:45:29.780 --> 00:45:30.280
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, so I--

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:32.410
<v Jaime Lowe>I've really come around to the shame concept.

00:45:32.410 --> 00:45:33.680
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, you've really been--

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:35.930
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm really embracing it.

00:45:35.930 --> 00:45:39.110
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, you have. That's true. That is true.

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:57.050
<v Jaime Lowe>We're joking, but there's something there. I'm realizing that, for me, shame is related to mental illness. After mania, it's hard not to want to be buried for a decade, until everyone forgets that you tried to start a hippie cult in a tutu covered in glitter and war paint.

00:45:57.050 --> 00:46:00.600
<v Jaime Lowe>Today, at the end of the session, my homework is a little different.

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:06.360
<v Debra Kaysen>And then I want you to practice giving one compliment and receiving one compliment.

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:08.190
<v Jaime Lowe>I don't really speak to people that much.

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:12.080
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, now I know. So it would mean you'd have to speak to people.

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:13.750
<v Jaime Lowe>Give one compliment, and--

00:46:13.750 --> 00:46:15.010
<v Debra Kaysen>And receive one compliment.

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:17.720
<v Jaime Lowe>How do I make someone compliment me?

00:46:17.720 --> 00:46:20.810
<v Debra Kaysen>So that's tough, because you're in a strange city.

00:46:20.810 --> 00:46:23.210
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:30.400
<v Debra Kaysen>So what it's going to mean is a couple of things. Either you're going to need to listen for compliments you're getting naturally without filtering them.

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:31.310
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:46:31.310 --> 00:46:37.820
<v Debra Kaysen>Or sometimes if you're interacting with people, then you may be more likely to get compliments.

00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:57.380
<v Jaime Lowe>This is the point of the exercise. Dr. Kaysen is preparing me for life post-treatment by encouraging me to interact with people. She explains that social isolation often follows PTSD.

00:46:57.380 --> 00:46:59.070
<v Jaime Lowe>Session nine.

00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:01.390
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. Well, let's dig in to how the practice went.

00:47:01.390 --> 00:47:02.300
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:08.840
<v Debra Kaysen>Before we go into worksheets, how did the giving and receiving compliments go?

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:22.010
<v Jaime Lowe>Yesterday when Dr. Kaysen gave me the compliment assignment, it seemed difficult and silly. It felt like I entered Mr. Roger's land. And even though I know he was a good soul, I was always a Muppets Show person.

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:36.170
<v Jaime Lowe>I wasn't entirely sure how to conjure up compliments. As I anticipated, giving compliments was pretty easy. I told Dr. Kaysen that I told my Airbnb guy I liked his record collection and his illustrations.

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:39.620
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, nice. Did you get any compliments?

00:47:39.620 --> 00:47:42.020
<v Jaime Lowe>I did. It was really funny, actually.

00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:42.840
<v Debra Kaysen>[LAUGHS]

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:48.530
<v Jaime Lowe>I got a very-- a really, really, really big compliment, to me.

00:47:48.530 --> 00:47:53.920
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.

00:47:53.920 --> 00:47:57.370
<v Jaime Lowe>I pause. I'm choking up.

00:47:57.370 --> 00:48:09.190
<v Jaime Lowe>[SNIFFS] Someone wrote me an email [SNIFFS] just about my book.

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:18.820
<v Ira Glass>About my book, Mental, the memoir I wrote about being bipolar. The email was from a young woman who is also bipolar.

00:48:18.820 --> 00:48:33.660
<v Jaime Lowe>She had to leave college, and go home, and kind of really shift her plans. And she said that she really felt like the story that I told was something that she had never read, but could really relate to. It was a really big compliment.

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.460
<v Debra Kaysen>That's powerful.

00:48:36.460 --> 00:48:46.940
<v Jaime Lowe>We'd been talking in a previous session about my feelings of being a failure, of not doing well at my work. Dr. Kaysen wants me to remember this email.

00:48:46.940 --> 00:48:57.580
<v Jaime Lowe>I can try and remember it. [LAUGHS] It's hard. It's really hard for me to remember the positive responses, like, to anything.

00:48:57.580 --> 00:49:03.390
<v Debra Kaysen>Yep. And so this is one for you to practice, right? So we're starting to move into life skills, right?

00:49:03.390 --> 00:49:03.760
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.

00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:21.280
<v Debra Kaysen>This is one that's going to be a life skill for you. What am I leaving out? That's been a really helpful question for you. Right? It forces you to get out of that negative space and look for things that don't fit with the stuck point.

00:49:21.280 --> 00:49:34.510
<v Jaime Lowe>This is the next to last session, and I can feel we're coming to the end. The way Dr. Kaysen is talking and the way I'm feeling, it just feels close to over. And then my last homework assignment is revealed.

00:49:34.510 --> 00:49:41.050
<v Debra Kaysen>I want you to write at least one page on what you think, now, about why the traumatic event occurred.

00:49:41.050 --> 00:49:41.770
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:49:41.770 --> 00:50:01.510
<v Ira Glass>It makes sense. If one purpose of this therapy is to change the story you're telling yourself, it would be a good gauge to write a before and after, to see how the narrative shifts-- the perfect book ends.

00:50:01.510 --> 00:50:04.000
<v Ira Glass>Session ten.

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:06.490
<v Debra Kaysen>How was it writing the second impact statement?

00:50:06.490 --> 00:50:10.930
<v Jaime Lowe>It was good. It felt like it was less fraught.

00:50:10.930 --> 00:50:57.420
<v Ira Glass>It's the day of my last session, and it's like Seattle knows it. This morning, I rode the ferry and saw a double rainbow. I walked around Bainbridge Island with a friend and saw sun, real sun through trees and abandoned mills, sun shining against the sound, and against graffiti, and against soaring seagulls, sun that reflected on the sign for Bernie's Automotive Service that read, "Welcome to the team, Charlie." It all looked shining and miraculous. I could feel the excitement of being done. And the Pacific Northwest felt it with me.

00:50:57.420 --> 00:51:05.970
<v Ira Glass>Writing the second impact statement was easier than writing the first one. I didn't feel pent up emotions spilling over. I didn't feel out of control.

00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:09.080
<v Debra Kaysen>Nice. So I'm going to have you read it to me.

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:09.400
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:51:09.400 --> 00:51:13.710
<v Debra Kaysen>OK?

00:51:13.710 --> 00:51:40.150
<v Jaime Lowe>The attack happened because-- who knows why it happened? I don't know why he molested me. I don't know what motivated him. I know that it didn't happen because I was there, or wearing boxers, or friendly, or willing it upon myself. I was young and walking to school, not looking for a sexual assault. That's pretty improbable. Why would anyone look for that?

00:51:40.150 --> 00:52:03.430
<v Jaime Lowe>I think it happened because-- I just really can't explain why. He was in control. He had a knife. I did everything I could to protect myself and to get away. My feelings after it happened were natural. My response was as good as it could be. Good is the wrong word, but I think I did OK, considering all of the circumstances.

00:52:03.430 --> 00:52:11.870
<v Ira Glass>I keep going. And when I get to the end, Dr. Kaysen reads back what I wrote after our first session, my original impact statement.

00:52:11.870 --> 00:52:14.040
<v Debra Kaysen>So let me read back to you what you wrote the first time.

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:18.500
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.

00:52:18.500 --> 00:52:34.620
<v Debra Kaysen>I was young, 13, and I don't think I knew much about actual human nature-- friendliness, strangers. It's funny to think that I was not cautious about the very thing kids are always warned of-- strangers. I walked to my bus stop alone every morning.

00:52:34.620 --> 00:52:39.180
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen keeps reading, and I can hear how my story has changed.

00:52:39.180 --> 00:52:41.820
<v Debra Kaysen>So what changes do you hear?

00:52:41.820 --> 00:52:49.130
<v Jaime Lowe>Well, I think there's a huge shift of focus. Now, it feels like there's just a different way of seeing it.

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:50.160
<v Debra Kaysen>Right.

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:55.390
<v Jaime Lowe>Like, that attack just doesn't feel as potent, I think.

00:52:55.390 --> 00:53:07.410
<v Debra Kaysen>Yep. Yep. That's what I heard, too. And also, really an acceptance that you may never know-- in fact, you probably will never know-- exactly why it happened. But you can be really confident that it didn't have anything to do with you.

00:53:07.410 --> 00:53:15.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Right. Which is something that I do think I intellectually understood, but it wasn't something I accepted, I don't think.

00:53:15.720 --> 00:53:16.230
<v Debra Kaysen>Right.

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:23.400
<v Jaime Lowe>And I think that in the difference between the first statement to the last, there was acceptance in that.

00:53:23.400 --> 00:53:51.660
<v Ira Glass>Acceptance that it wasn't me, that it wasn't my fault. Dr. Kaysen hands me a print-out of the PTSD check-ins that we did at the beginning of each session. By the end, my number has dropped from the initial total by about 12 points. She says 10 points indicates meaningful change. The number doesn't mean that much to me, but I do feel better. I know that. She wonders how I feel about the therapy, overall.

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:58.180
<v Debra Kaysen>What did you notice in the process? What was helpful for you? What was not helpful? How have you seen shifts?

00:53:58.180 --> 00:54:27.840
<v Jaime Lowe>Well, I've definitely seen-- I mean, I think working through all of this stuff about the assault was incredibly helpful, because I felt like there was a lot of unresolved assumptions that I didn't really even recognize were there. And that it was, frankly, kind of a relief to not really have that there as much. Like it just kind of feels better.

00:54:27.840 --> 00:55:00.670
<v Ira Glass>The process of CPT surprised me. Its effectiveness surprised me. I could see in just those two statements how the ten sessions in between shifted my thinking. This is like ninja therapy. They distract you with the weird language, and then progress creeps up on you from behind. As always, Dr. Kaysen is huggable, gracious, warm. I'm going to miss her. I'm sad to leave, even though I know it's time, and I know I can.

00:55:00.670 --> 00:55:01.500
<v Jaime Lowe>Is that it?

00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:02.070
<v Debra Kaysen>That's it.

00:55:02.070 --> 00:55:03.130
<v Jaime Lowe>That's the end?

00:55:03.130 --> 00:55:04.790
<v Debra Kaysen>The end.

00:55:04.790 --> 00:55:14.400
<v Jaime Lowe>All right.

00:55:14.400 --> 00:55:34.880
<v Ira Glass>It's been 10 months since I did CPT. I still have my folder stuffed with more than 100 worksheets. The spine is reinforced with neon orange duct tape, because the folder is falling apart, but I'm not. I feel like I did, in fact, get something I wanted out of this, more than what I expected.

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:59.410
<v Ira Glass>I don't feel as hopeless and incapacitated when I hear about other assaults. The news doesn't dictate my emotional state in the same way. When Jeffrey Epstein was found dead, I was angry on behalf of his victims. I shouted a few expletives and threw my phone on the ground, but I could still function. And really, that's a very rational reaction.

00:55:59.410 --> 00:56:26.720
<v Ira Glass>Before CPT, I had a lot of anxiety and overwhelming feelings. I was experiencing PTSD symptoms, which I didn't even realize. I had my suspicions that these intense emotions were related to the assault, but it was never named for me. Now, my symptoms are mostly gone. And when problems come up, I can work them through in my head, or I pull out a blank worksheet.

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:59.230
<v Ira Glass>There's a surge of women seeking help right now. A National Sexual Assault hotline saw a 200% increase over normal volume after the Kavanaugh hearing. There was a 20% increase in calls after the R. Kelly doc aired. After the sentencing hearing of Larry Nassar, calls to the same hotline increased by 46%. Of course, so many survivors of sexual assault don't get any treatment at all-- not talk therapy, not CPT, nothing.

00:56:59.230 --> 00:57:26.350
<v Ira Glass>When I started CPT, it was hard to say I'd been sexually assaulted out loud. I think I carried a lot of shame-- the word I was most offended by. But now I can say it, and I can say it without crying. I was sexually assaulted when I was 13.

00:57:26.350 --> 00:57:36.120
<v Ira Glass>Jaime Lowe, she's the author of a memoir called Mental. In the month since she saw Dr. Debra Kaysen, Dr. Kaysen has moved from the University of Washington to Stanford.

00:57:36.120 --> 00:58:04.120
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - MARY J. BLIGE, "THERAPY"]

00:58:04.120 --> 00:58:24.210
